<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chapter-arithmetic">
  <title>Arithmetic</title>
  <section xml:id="arithmetic-intro">
    <title>Introduction</title>
    <introduction><p>Addition and subtraction combine or compare quantities; multiplication and division describe equal groups, scaling and sharing. An expression may contain more than one operation, so grouping and the agreed order of operations matter.</p></introduction>
    <p>The commutative, associative and distributive properties explain why many different calculation methods produce the same answer.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="arithmetic-practice">
    <title>Worked Example and Practice</title>
    <example xml:id="example-arithmetic">
      <title>Worked example</title>
      <statement><p>Evaluate 7+3×4.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>Multiplication is performed first: 7+12=19.</p></solution>
    </example>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-arithmetic">
      <statement><p>Evaluate (7+3)×4 and explain why it differs.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>The brackets group the addition first, giving 10×4=40.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>
</chapter>
